From e9820616ec32c271e910fbfef170cd623bb9e213 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FileZilla dark mode port <fzdark@localhost>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:43:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] build: ignore autoreconf scratch output

The build script now runs autoreconf -f -i, which rewrites the vendored
config/config.guess, config/config.sub, config/ltmain.sh, m4/libtool.m4,
src/fzshellext/configure and the aux scripts in place on every build. Add the
scratch it leaves behind (autom4te.cache, the *~ backups) to the ignore list,
and note why the modified vendored copies are deliberately left uncommitted
rather than folded into the patch series.

As with the block above, this one is inserted well clear of the CRLF-terminated
last line of the upstream file so that git am, which normalises that CR away via
git mailinfo, can still match the hunk.
---
 .gitignore | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+)

diff --git a/.gitignore b/.gitignore
index 4108e87..bfa1482 100644
--- a/.gitignore
+++ b/.gitignore
@@ -39,6 +39,32 @@ libtool
 locales/*.mo
 locales/*.po.new
 
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Autoreconf scratch.
+#
+# The build script runs autoreconf -f -i before configure, so configure,
+# config/config.guess, config/config.sub, config/ltmain.sh, m4/libtool.m4 and
+# src/fzshellext/configure get rewritten in place on every build. Those are
+# tracked upstream, so they show up as modifications here; they are left
+# unmodified in git rather than committed, because the patch series only ever
+# touches real sources and lets autoreconf regenerate the rest.
+#
+# The -f is deliberate. The tarball ships a prebuilt configure generated from
+# an older m4/wxwin.m4 carrying a hard "must use wxWidgets 3.2.x" gate, and a
+# configure restored from git has a fresh enough timestamp that plain
+# autoreconf -i would leave that stale copy in place.
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+autom4te.cache/
+*~
+
+# autoreconf refreshes the vendored aux scripts, which then differ from the
+# versions in git. Ignore the churn so the status of a real source edit stands
+# out.
+/config/compile
+/config/depcomp
+/config/install-sh
+/config/missing
+
 # autotools / automake build output
 Makefile
 Makefile.in
